00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.940
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.190
to go back to my application.

00:09:57.190 --> 00:09:58.110
I'm going to do--

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:01.330
just bootstrap a little bit
more functionality here, OK?

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.540
So I'm going to go ahead
and create a hello world

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:05.860
controller here.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.290
Let me just make sure.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.950
Yeah, I'm going to say hello.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.940
And it's going to be
a risk controller.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.830
And I'm going to map a
URL here called /hello.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:18.107
I'm going to pass in the
name here with name string.

00:10:18.107 --> 00:10:18.940
I'm going to log in.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.910
I'm going to return hello back
to whoever said hello to me.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:24.640
And I'm just going to go
ahead and use private val here

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:28.480
to inject the personRepository
that I just created now.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.090
And what I'm going to do is, in
addition to saying hello back,

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:32.980
I'm going to use
the personRepository

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:35.050
and save the data
back into this.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.200
So I'm going to say
name is equal to name.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.250
And it's something
like that, OK?

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.110
And hopefully, that
will just work.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:44.320
Oh, but wait-- to simulate
that, we have multiple services

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.270
to code each other,
because you usually

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:47.560
don't have just one service.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.700
I don't have time to
create two of them today.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.242
But what I'm going to do is to
just simulate this whole thing

00:10:52.242 --> 00:10:53.620
by making another
endpoint that's

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.510
going to call a few names here.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.150
I'm going to call Mike, who is
the TO on the Spring Cloud GCP

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.230
project; Mindy, who is
one of my colleagues

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.620
that I first worked with
when I joined Cloud; Jisha,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:05.500
who is also awesome, and
she contributed a lot

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:06.307
to this project.

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:08.140
I'm going to say hello
to each of the names.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:10.570
I'm going to use the
restTemplate client

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.710
to make a call to
myself to say hello

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.130
so we can simulate calling
another service, right?

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.310
Just 'cause I don't have
time to write two of them.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.070
And so to do this, I need
to go ahead and, of course,

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.470
go ahead and just make sure
we have a RestTemplate Bean

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:24.950
that we can bootstrap.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.040
All these are pretty
idiomatic to Spring.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.490
And I need to go ahead and
automatically inject this

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:32.480
into my controller as well.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.700
So I'm going to go ahead and
do a private val rest template

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.103
and just inject it here.

00:11:38.103 --> 00:11:39.520
So now, I have two
more endpoints.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.000
I have /hello, which
I will save the data.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.838
I'm going to say
hello all, which

00:11:43.838 --> 00:11:45.130
will say hello to three people.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:47.280
And it will save each
of the data as well.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.330
And I'm going to go ahead and
just restart this application.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.940
I hope that's going
to just work as well.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:54.830
So I'm going to stop and run.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:55.460
Whoo!

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.640
And let me see, just make sure
this is actually rebooting.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.320
Yeah, very good.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:03.730
And I'm going to go back
to the test HTTP here.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.888
And let's just do a
few more things, OK?

00:12:05.888 --> 00:12:07.430
OK, that also started
pretty quickly.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:08.650
So hopefully, this works.

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:13.930
So I'm going to say hello
to localhost 8080/hello.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.990
I'm going to say name is NEXT.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:17.860
Let's do that.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.300
And let's run it.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.380
Say hello to NEXT.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.470
And of course, we
get a hello back.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.300
And remember, we
also saved the data.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.730
And just to show you
that this is actually

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.920
saving the data
now, we can see this

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.350
up on the screen in
the Datastore, right?

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:33.850
And I can also say hello to all.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.950
So rather than saying
hello to just one person,

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.053
I'm going to say hello to all.

00:12:38.053 --> 00:12:39.970
So it's going to iterate
through and say hello

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.710
to three different people.

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:44.320
And if we go back to
the log, we should

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.190
be able to see that we say
hello to Mike, Mindy, and Jisha.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.180
And if we go back
to the Datastore,

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:52.190
we should be able to see all
of the people I said hello to,

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:52.690
right?

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:55.780
So it's very rapid that you can
develop your application very

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:57.640
quickly now.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.440
So what's next?

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.650
In a microservices
application architecture,

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.470
if you do have all of these
services calling each other,

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.690
A calls B calls C, you
kind of need some way

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:10.240
to observe and
kind of understand,

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.493
what is the call stack
across all of your services?

00:13:12.493 --> 00:13:14.660
And to do that, you may
need to add some distributed

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:15.685
tracing capabilities.

00:13:15.685 --> 00:13:17.560
And there are a number
of libraries out there

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:21.430
you can use, like OpenTracing,
OpenSenses, and Brave,

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:23.260
and so on, and so on.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.690
In Spring, we have
Spring Cloud Sleuth,

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:28.270
which can actually add
distributed tracing capability

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.330
to your app.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:31.840
Now, regardless of whether
you deploy your application

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.265
into a Istio environment, which
can trace things for you--

00:13:35.265 --> 00:13:36.640
regardless of
whether you do that

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:39.640
or whether you just deploy it
in plain Kubernetes or VMs,

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:42.400
you always need to
propagate the trace ID.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.660
You always have to do that.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.480
And Spring Cloud Sleuth or
some of these tracing libraries

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.150
can capture the trace ID for
you and propagate it for you

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:50.082
automatically.

00:13:50.082 --> 00:13:51.790
Now, for our demo,
what we're going to do

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:55.510
is go even a step further by
adding a tracing starter here

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.520
that we created for
Spring Cloud GCP.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.350
What this will do is to build
on top of Spring Cloud Sleuth,

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:01.750
which is built on top of Brave.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:03.640
What this will do is to
automatically capture

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:04.660
the trace IDs.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.452
If not, it's going to
generate one for you.

00:14:06.452 --> 00:14:08.368
And it's also going to
automatically propagate

00:14:08.368 --> 00:14:10.120
it downstream to the
next service call

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.800
when you're using RestTemplate.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.630
And we're also going
to automatically

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:17.720
propagate all the traces
into Stackdriver Trace, OK?

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:20.470
And we just done that
by adding the starter.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.870
We don't have to add any
other additional code.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.090
The only other thing
that I might actually add

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.490
is the probability of
a trace to be stored.

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:30.820
By default, we only capture 10%
of the traces or the requests

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.800
where we're going
to store the trace.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.920
For the demo, I'm going to
up it to 1.0, which is 100%,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:38.410
so we can actually
see everything

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:39.700
that's coming through.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.950
Now, in addition to doing
this, what I also want to do

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:45.520
is to log my messages
into Stackdriver as well.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.290
So we have a logging
starter for that.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:50.740
What this will do is to
allow you to add a log back

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.230
configuration fairly easily.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.930
And you can then
bootstrap your application

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:59.683
that will understand how to
log into Stackdriver Logging.

00:14:59.683 --> 00:15:01.600
And there's a reason why
I'm doing this, which

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.040
I'll show you in a second.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.650
So let me just go
ahead and do this one.

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:08.210
I'm going to use this
template for now.

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.570
So what this will do is to
log the message to the console

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:13.750
so I can see it and SD out.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.940
But I'm also going to log it to
Stackdriver as the other API.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:19.570
So now, we have
two log appenders.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.070
It's going to split
the log message.

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:22.990
And we're going to
simultaneously send it

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.670
to both places.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:27.100
So let me go ahead and
just restart this app

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:30.250
and see if this is
hooked up properly.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:33.760
And hopefully, we'll get to
see the traces and the log

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:34.660
messages.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.735
And if you've seen so far,
there's not so much code,

00:15:37.735 --> 00:15:39.610
because Spring and Spring
Boot, the framework

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:42.140
itself is doing a lot of the
work behind the scenes for you,

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:42.850
OK?

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:44.480
So it's all started.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.755
So let's go ahead and
go back to my test.

00:15:47.755 --> 00:15:50.150
And I'm going to
say hello to all.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:53.670
Now, ideally, what happened here
is that I trigger this request.

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.340
The trace ID is generated.

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.390
It's propagated downstream
via the REST template

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.320
to the next call.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:15:59.380
We have three calls.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.800
So all of them are
correlated together.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.690
If I actually go back to the
run, if I look at the log,

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.760
now, not only do
we see the names,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.260
if I scroll left
a little bit, we

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.780
can confirm that here's
a trace ID that's been

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:14.430
across three different calls.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.940
And each of the calls is
a span that has a span ID.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:20.790
And they're all being sampled,
meaning that they are all being

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.250
stored into Stackdriver Trace.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:27.420
So what that means is that if
I go here, and go to Trace,

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:32.100
and see the Trace list, I have
this one additional entry that

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.060
maybe just came in.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:34.150
Yeah, there it is.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.610
And we can see that we
call localhost 8080/all.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:41.760
We can see the three
different REST template calls

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.700
to the remote services,
which is myself.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.250
And we can see how long
each of those take.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:49.950
And furthermore, because
we appended a log,

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:51.920
we sent a log to
Stackdriver Logging

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.660
along with a trace
ID and a span ID,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:57.990
we can actually do this, which
is show you correlated logging.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.090
So because all of
these ID tie up,

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.118
I can actually go and see, OK,
what happened in this span?

00:17:03.118 --> 00:17:04.910
What kind of error
messages came out of it?

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:08.319
They're all correlated together,
which is really, really useful

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:10.890
if you're actually trying to
debug some of the production

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:12.990
environment issues, OK?

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:16.380
So that's pretty much it
for my service for now.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.630
What do we do next?

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.829
Let's go ahead and deploy it
into my Kubernetes cluster

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:22.589
in this case.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:25.180
Now, we have, of course, many,
many ways to deploy this.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.210
We can deploy this into
App Engine as a JAR

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:29.460
in the new App Engine runtime.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.080
We can deploy this into
Cloud Run as a container.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:33.940
We can deploy this
into Kubernetes,

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:35.370
whether it has Istio or not.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:36.810
It doesn't really matter.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.720
So we're just going to take
the long route of, let's

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.630
deploy everything
into Kubernetes.

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:43.830
And I'll show you all
the intrinsic details

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:45.850
that we have to
care about when we

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:49.410
are running a Java application
inside of a container.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:51.720
So what we're going to do
first is to containerize.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.320
And we're going to talk a little
bit here about containerizing--

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.580
can I not spell today?

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.270
Containerize, there we go.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:02.150
And then we're going to talk a
little bit about the liveness

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.520
checks and readiness checks.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:07.170
And we need to
talk about, how do

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:10.370
you listen to the graceful
shutdown events that

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:12.840
is a contract with the
Kubernetes runtime,

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:16.140
where in any Docker
environments that you actually

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.210
have a restriction
tool, you want to be

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:20.220
able to shut down gracefully.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:22.560
And then finally, we're to
look at monitoring as well.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.410
How do you export metrics from
this particular application

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.330
so that we can see
it from Prometheus

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.012
dashboards in this setting?

00:18:29.012 --> 00:18:31.470
And if we have time, we're
going to see how we can actually

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.012
debug this application
and production

00:18:33.012 --> 00:18:36.120
environment in a Kubernetes
environment as well.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.650
So we're going to
get through that,

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.440
hopefully, in the
next 30 minutes or so.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.980
So let's get started.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.150
Now, when you're containerizing
this application,

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.452
you have to be very careful.

00:18:47.452 --> 00:18:49.410
I don't know if anyone
here ran into this issue

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.030
where you containerize
the Java application,

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:53.155
you're running your Kubernetes
cluster or some kind

00:18:53.155 --> 00:18:56.070
of environment, and then you
get this weird message called

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:58.890
Out Of Memory
killed, OOM killed.

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:00.570
A lot of people run
into this issue.

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:04.560
That is mostly because the
JVM runtime may or may not

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.570
understand how much
resource you actually

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.827
assigned to your container.

00:19:08.827 --> 00:19:10.410
And to take care of
this, you actually

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.400
have to understand which
Java version, which

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:15.340
OpenJDK version you're using.

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:18.540
And what type of containerized
support does it actually have?

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:20.130
Because often
enough, what happened

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.070
is that the OpenJDK
runtime does not actually

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:26.670
know how much memory is being
assigned to your container.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:29.940
And the JVM, which has used
up as much memory as possible,

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.610
and ultimately, you're just
going to get OOM killed.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:36.243
And your application will just
be restarting all the time.

00:19:36.243 --> 00:19:38.160
So first, we need to go
ahead and containerize

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:39.300
this application.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:42.240
Now, I can write a Docker file.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:43.940
I can write a very simple one.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.050
But I actually have a
separate talk in the past,

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:48.840
just for a whole hour, on
how to write a proper Docker

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.440
file that will
containerize your Java app.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:53.700
There is a lot of details
we have to go through.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:55.740
Now, luckily enough,
we do actually now

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.150
have a plugin called jib.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:00.060
So if you're not using jib
already, please take a look.

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:01.800
This is a Maven
or Gradle plugin.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.840
You can just add it
to your build file.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:07.150
And from here on out, I can just
use it to build my container.

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:08.650
I don't need to
write a Docker file.

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:11.040
You'll actually follow
some of the best practices.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.140
And you don't even
need Docker installed

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:14.100
on your local machine.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.440
You'll just push
the image directly

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.360
to the remote registry.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:20.870
So I'm going to go ahead and
create a new image here called

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:21.370
gcr.io.

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:26.640
