00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:52.620
in a containerized environment.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.920
We have to deal with Kubernetes
in the Kubernetes contracts

00:23:55.920 --> 00:24:00.300
in terms of how this application
will behave in the lifecycle.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.450
What that means is, when
the application starts,

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:04.330
there's a lightning
strike that might go off.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:06.580
We need to be able to check
whether the application is

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:07.463
still alive.

00:24:07.463 --> 00:24:09.630
If an application is not
responding to the lightning

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:13.020
strike, Kubernetes will have
to restart it and automatically

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:14.115
try to fix this issue.

00:24:14.115 --> 00:24:15.990
The other thing that we
have to watch out for

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:17.540
is the readiness check, right?

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:19.108
In Kubernetes, we
have two checks.

00:24:19.108 --> 00:24:21.150
Liveness check just
determines whether Kubernetes

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:23.682
needs to kill this instance
and restart it or not.

00:24:23.682 --> 00:24:25.140
The readiness check
is the one that

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:27.780
determines whether the traffic
will be falling through

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:30.990
to my container, to my pod.

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:34.150
So we need-- it's ideal
to have both checks, OK?

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:35.910
You definitely should have both.

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:39.220
Now, the readiness check is
really useful in two ways.

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:41.280
One is that your
application could be alive,

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:43.200
but it may not be
ready to serve.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:43.700
Why?

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:45.533
Because you might need
to warm up the cache.

00:24:45.533 --> 00:24:48.120
You have to make sure all the
connections are established,

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:49.860
everything's ready
to go before you

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:53.550
flip the readiness check over
and say, give me some traffic.

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.388
The other use case of it is
that when you are shutting down

00:24:56.388 --> 00:24:58.680
the application, when Kubernetes
is trying to shut down

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:00.180
your Java application,
it's going

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.250
to send you a SIGTERM signal.

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:03.680
It's a termination signal.

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:05.880
And you are supposed
to catch it and then do

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:07.260
a graceful shutdown.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:09.870
During this whole
process, ideally, you

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:11.580
should turn off
the readiness check

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:15.180
so that you tell Kubernetes,
I got the message.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:16.290
I'm going to shut down.

00:25:16.290 --> 00:25:19.570
Please don't send
me any more traffic.

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:21.090
And you use that
to tell Kubernetes

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:24.220
whether you are going to
receive traffic or not.

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:26.440
And once you have gracefully
shut down and served

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:29.490
all the in-flight requests,
you shut down your application.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:30.750
And everything's fine.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.300
So we don't have to do
both of them right now.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:36.030
For the liveness check,
the easiest way to do that

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:40.380
is just by adding a simple
handler here that would just

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.250
respond OK all the time, right?

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:43.860
So I'm going to say, am I alive?

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.480
And it's going to say "ok."

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:49.080
And I'm going to map
it to maybe /alive.

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:50.890
That's really easily done.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.480
So I just added a
liveness check here.

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:57.070
The other thing that we may want
to add is the readiness check.

00:25:57.070 --> 00:26:00.460
The way that I think
it's pretty awesome thing

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:02.530
to do within the
Spring ecosystem

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:05.480
is actually by using
something called the actuator.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:07.510
So Spring has something
called the Spring

00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:12.180
Actuator that will expose a
health checkpoint for you.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:14.440
So I'm going to go ahead
and add the actuator--

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:16.670
actuator right here.

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:20.920
It's going to expose a /actuator
endpoint with a health endpoint

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:22.960
there that you can
actually plug in.

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:25.480
And you can use
that to kind of flip

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:27.640
yes or no between whether
your application is

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:29.680
ready or not, OK?

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:32.410
And then there are many
different ways for you

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:35.320
to do a graceful shutdown.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.410
The gist here is that you have
to listen for the termination

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:39.120
signal.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.930
In Java, the way you
do it's by listening

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:42.280
to runtime shutdown events.

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:44.770
And then you have a separate
thread that can react to it.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:47.570
And then that's where
you respond to the--

00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:49.430
and shut down your application.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.850
There's actually a really
nice module out there

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:54.180
that can be useful for you.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:59.090
It's the graceful shutdown
artifact from SBB CH here.

00:26:59.090 --> 00:27:05.110
What that is going to do
is to allow you to replace

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:07.120
how you start your application.

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:10.270
So rather than using the Spring
Boot standard application,

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:14.340
I'm going to go ahead and
