00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:27.290
redeploy this file for me.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:29.840
So anytime you make some
changes in your source tree,

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:31.850
Skaffold will pick
up the new changes

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:33.200
and push it to Kubernetes.

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:35.420
And because of this,
we should actually

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:39.110
have a new service now that's
being provisioned called

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:40.820
demoservice.

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:44.000
Oh, one thing I forgot to do is
to make this same demoservice

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:45.390
so that we actually get an IP.

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.620
So let me go in and fix.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.820
So let me go down here.

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:52.900
And for the load balancer
for the type-- uh-oh,

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:54.430
where's the type?

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:58.120
Let me do the type here, type.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:58.990
I'm going to say--

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:02.360
oh, yeah-- type
is a LoadBalancer.

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:03.160
Let's do that.

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:06.100
And that will get us a external
IP address so we can actually

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:08.580
test it out, OK?

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:10.000
So what have we done so far?

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.680
We created a
brand-new application.

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:13.110
We containerized
it, understanding

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:14.920
what JVM version to use.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:16.830
We added liveness check
and readiness check.

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:18.480
We added graceful shutdown.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:22.020
And we're now changing
things, now, with scaffold

00:32:22.020 --> 00:32:24.930
so that we can test this thing
out in Kubernetes, hopefully,

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.460
very quickly.

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:30.480
And now we have added a load
balancer with an external IP.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:34.200
So I'm just going to wait for
this IP to become available.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:38.487
Then we should be able to see
this whole thing in Kubernetes.

00:32:38.487 --> 00:32:39.570
Not too bad so far, right?

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:41.430
It's OK, yeah?

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:43.200
All right, so the
next part of this

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:46.950
is to actually add the
deployment, modify it

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:49.460
so that we take advantage
of the liveness checks

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:52.260
and the readiness checks,
and a few things that we

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.120
have to watch out for
when you want to do

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:55.890
a zero-downtime deployment.

00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:57.990
So first thing first
is the livenessProbe.

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:00.960
We already have
this on port 8080.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:06.970
And we can also ping it
on the path of /alive.

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:08.410
That's where we put it.

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:10.540
And then we have the
readiness probe that

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:14.280
is also right now on port 8080.

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:18.717
And the path is actually
/actuator/health.

00:33:18.717 --> 00:33:21.300
Now, one of the annoying things
that you have to be careful of

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:23.290
is that, remember,
we added tracing.

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:25.780
So trace-- tracing at 100%.

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:28.660
So what that means is every
time I get a health check,

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:31.710
I'm going to see a little
dot on my trace list

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:34.320
for the liveness check.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:37.170
And that's sometimes
not so useful, right?

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:40.740
So we can actually turn that off
in the liveness probe as well.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:43.740
The way we're going to do it
is by adding the hb header.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:46.650
Now, because we're using
Sleuth behind the scenes,

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:49.630
it uses what we call
the x-b3 headers.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:54.030
So we can use the x-b3
header called x-b3-sampled.

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.640
And we can turn this value to 0.

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:01.170
Then, you will not get any more
traces for this endpoint that's

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:02.680
coming from the liveness probe.

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:04.440
This is actually really useful.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:06.480
Otherwise, you're going
to see a lot of traces,

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:09.630
such as the liveness probe,
which may not help you a lot,

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:10.395
at all, right?

00:34:10.395 --> 00:34:12.020
So I'm going to go
ahead and save this.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:17.010
And again, this is going to
kick off the new deployment.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:17.699
There you go.

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:20.739
And it's going to apply my
changes and so on and so forth.

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:23.620
So yeah, hopefully,
that's all working.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:25.920
So let's see if the
IP came back on.

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:26.820
So there it is.

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:28.909
So there's 35, 22, whatever.

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:32.265
And I'm going to go
ahead and try it out.

00:34:32.265 --> 00:34:34.641
Let's see if that works.

00:34:34.641 --> 00:34:36.909
Oh, OK, not bad, yeah.

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:39.550
OK, so we can also
see the actuator

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.800
right now, which is in health.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:42.760
And oh, look.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:43.840
This is actually down.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:45.130
This is cool, right?

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:48.250
Because we have the readiness
probe that we configured.

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:50.170
Remember, I changed
the deployment file.

00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:52.030
So it's actually doing
a rolling update.

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:54.040
So as it's doing
the rolling update,

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:57.010
it sends a SIGTERM signal
to my existing instance.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:59.820
And so now, my readiness
probe is being turned down.

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:01.990
And Kubernetes does
take a couple of seconds

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:04.130
to catch up to this status.

00:35:04.130 --> 00:35:06.200
So that's why the thing
is still going through.

00:35:06.200 --> 00:35:08.950
But eventually, this thing
will go out of service.

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:11.260
And then now, I'm back
to the new instance

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:14.650
of my app with the
new configuration, OK?

00:35:14.650 --> 00:35:16.090
Pretty awesome.

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:17.770
Now, all at the
same time, we still

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.260
have to log messages and
everything else that's here.

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:22.758
Now, of course, in Kubernetes,
when you deploy something

00:35:22.758 --> 00:35:24.550
to Kubernetes, one of
the things you really

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:26.770
have to watch out for,
regardless of whether it's

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:29.590
run in App Engine, or
Kubernetes, please do not write

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:31.390
logs to the file system.

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:32.890
Just please don't do that.

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:37.230
You either do stdout or
you write it to the API.

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:43.130
And in GKE, what you can do is
to output a log as JSON format.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.030
So rather than sending
it via the API,

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:47.802
you can actually do
something like this.

00:35:47.802 --> 00:35:49.510
So I'm going to go
ahead and change this.

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:54.490
And I'm going to use JSON
logging instead, log JSON.

00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:57.610
So by changing out
the appender, we

00:35:57.610 --> 00:36:00.760
are now able to log
things into JSON messages.

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:02.620
And Stackdriver
has the agent that

00:36:02.620 --> 00:36:03.880
can automatically parse it.

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:06.880
And you get the same benefits
of getting the log levels,

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:10.123
and the trace IDs, and span
IDs, and everything else.

00:36:10.123 --> 00:36:12.040
And as you can see, every
time I do something,

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:13.850
it's going to try
to rebuild my app.

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:16.330
If you have tests, it
will run the tests.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:18.820
And it will redeploy
this whole thing.

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:21.460
Now, the last piece of
this is that I really

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:23.955
want to also add
monitoring to this, right?

00:36:23.955 --> 00:36:25.705
I want to be able to
see how many requests

00:36:25.705 --> 00:36:27.070
is coming through.

00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:28.445
How long do the requests take?

00:36:28.445 --> 00:36:30.070
And the most important
thing in the JVM

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:32.830
is how much memory
I'm using in the heap.

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:37.210
And also, potentially, how
many threads am I running?

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:39.310
There are many ways
for you to get this.

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:41.230
If you have Prometheus,
you can write--

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:43.150
you can use a Prometheus agent.

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:45.070
Because I'm in Spring,
what I'm going to do

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:47.860
is to use what we call
the Micrometer, which

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:50.450
is another open-source
library that's out there.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:56.180
So by adding Micrometer like
this with a Prometheus adapter,

00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:58.310
in conjunction
with the actuator,

00:36:58.310 --> 00:37:02.060
what this will do is to
potentially expose your matrix

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:06.350
onto an endpoint called
/actuator/prometheus.

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:09.390
And if you actually
have this configured,

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:12.860
then we can actually scrape
the Prometheus metrics

00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:15.320
and automatically forward
that to Stackdriver Trace,

00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:16.980
Stackdriver Monitoring as well.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.280
And we can actually
graph and plot out

00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:21.547
all of these different
metrics for you.

00:37:21.547 --> 00:37:22.880
So this is what I'm going to do.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:25.490
I'm going to go ahead and
just make sure that we

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.790
have the management.exposure.

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:30.090
Yeah, there we go.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:34.540
So we want to expose the health
endpoint and the Prometheus

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:36.895
output here.

00:37:36.895 --> 00:37:38.270
However, when you
do this, you've

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:40.490
got to be careful,
because now, anybody

00:37:40.490 --> 00:37:42.560
who has access to
that load balancer

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:44.363
can actually hit these
endpoints, right?

00:37:44.363 --> 00:37:45.780
So you're going
to see the health,

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:47.240
whether your application
is up or not.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:49.160
You're going to see your
Prometheus stats and stuff

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:49.800
like that.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:52.670
So you got to take care
of securing those URLs.

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:54.470
And you can do that
within the framework.

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:57.320
You can do that via
a proxy of some sort.

00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:59.060
Or this is what I'm
going to do instead.

00:37:59.060 --> 00:38:01.730
I'm going to go ahead
and put my actuator--

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:03.645
like this metadata information--

00:38:03.645 --> 00:38:05.270
I'm going to put it
on a separate port.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:08.330
I'm going to put it on
port 8081 instead, OK?

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:11.360
So I'm still serving my
application via port 8080.

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:13.340
My liveness check is
still on that port.

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:17.660
But then my readiness check,
my Prometheus information,

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:21.410
that's all going to be on the
internal port 8081 instead.

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:24.350
So we can definitely do
that right there, OK?

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:25.970
So let me go ahead and do that.

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:27.920
And then, of course, I
have to go back in here

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:31.640
and change out the
readiness probe to 8081.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:35.900
If I want to use Prometheus
to scrape my application's

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:39.170
monitoring metrics, there
are a number of ways for you

00:38:39.170 --> 00:38:40.580
to do it.

00:38:40.580 --> 00:38:43.970
Mostly, you probably install
a Prometheus operator.

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:46.340
In my case, I just install
a bare minimum version

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:47.180
of Prometheus.

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:49.520
And then we added the
Stackdriver sidecar to it

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:53.330
so that we can automatically
forward the monitoring metrics

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:54.710
to Stackdriver Trace.

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.740
And for that to happen, I
do need to add a few more

00:38:57.740 --> 00:38:58.770
annotations here.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:00.440
So I'm going to
add an annotation.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:04.250
And to tell Prometheus to
actually scrape my endpoints,

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:07.190
I'm going to say,
prometheus.io annotation scrape

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:08.788
is equal to true.

00:39:08.788 --> 00:39:10.580
And I'm going to do
the similar thing here.

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:12.290
We need to know
which port to scrape.

00:39:12.290 --> 00:39:14.030
So it's going to be
8081, because that's

00:39:14.030 --> 00:39:15.590
where the endpoint is.

00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:18.590
And then finally, I'm going to
do prometheus.io of path, which

00:39:18.590 --> 00:39:20.590
is going to be actuator/path.

00:39:20.590 --> 00:39:22.010
Oh, sorry, not that one--

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:24.220
like that, OK?

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:27.910
So again, I'm doing this in
the Skaffold development loop.

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.560
It's going to detect the
change in my deployment.

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:32.470
It's going to go ahead
and redeploy, now,

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:34.720
with the annotation, with
Prometheus scraping and all

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:35.430
that.

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.660
And we can see how this
whole thing kind of ties

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:39.010
up end to end.

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:41.320
And once my application
comes back up,

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.670
I should still be able
to access my app, right?

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:49.420
And then we should be able to go
ahead and go to the Monitoring

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:52.000
console to see what's
actually there.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:53.115
So let's give it a try.

00:39:53.115 --> 00:39:54.730
Oh, not like that.

00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:55.970
So here we go.

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:58.570
So here's the endpoint
with the persons.

00:39:58.570 --> 00:40:02.050
I'm going to go ahead and
say hello to all again.

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:03.940
So I'm going to say hello all.

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:05.650
And it's going to go
through this thing

00:40:05.650 --> 00:40:08.140
with saying hello to
three people or so.

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:09.790
I can go back to my Trace.

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:11.530
And I'm going to
turn on auto reload.

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:15.520
And ideally, I should
be able to see the--

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:17.230
yeah, I can see my traces.

00:40:17.230 --> 00:40:20.050
Now, this is actually happening
from Kubernetes cluster

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:23.470
with the logs outputted as JSON
and correlated to my Trace.

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:28.920
So everything works as expected
in my Kubernetes environment.

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:33.062
And for monitoring,
what I can do

00:40:33.062 --> 00:40:35.020
is then go to Stackdriver
Monitoring, because I

00:40:35.020 --> 00:40:36.680
have Prometheus all set up.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:38.480
I can go to
Stackdriver Monitoring.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:42.290
And I can hopefully
use my metrics export.

00:40:42.290 --> 00:40:45.560
And I should be up to find
the metrics I want to see.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:47.540
So here, we can go to Resources.

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:49.850
We can go ahead and--

00:40:49.850 --> 00:40:53.610
there we go-- go to
the Metrics Explorer.

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:57.160
And I should be able to
see Kubernetes Container--

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:58.370
yeah, that one.

00:40:58.370 --> 00:41:02.140
And then all of
these scraped metrics

00:41:02.140 --> 00:41:06.270
is going to go into a metric
called external/prometheus--

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:09.030
Prometheus, yeah--
and then the suffix

00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:10.690
of the actual metrics itself.

00:41:10.690 --> 00:41:13.230
So for example, if I want
to see the server request--

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:15.290
so I can see request counts.

00:41:15.290 --> 00:41:15.900
Where is it?

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:17.320
Right there.

00:41:17.320 --> 00:41:20.540
And we can plot this
out from my application.

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:26.545
And I go-- so Group By, of
course, by the name of the pod.

00:41:26.545 --> 00:41:28.920
And as you can see, I already
have some other application

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:29.462
running here.

00:41:29.462 --> 00:41:32.190
You can see the metrics
from it, because the agent

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:33.070
is running right now.

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:35.760
We just need some time
for it to catch up, right?

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.925
The other thing that's also
very useful is the JVM metric.

00:41:38.925 --> 00:41:41.130
So I'm going to say jvm_memory.

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:43.880
And we can see memory
used in that one.

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:47.090
That one's super useful.

00:41:47.090 --> 00:41:53.060
And let me go ahead and group
by this by the name of the pod

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:55.440
and by name of the
container as well.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:57.200
Now, you can already see--

00:41:57.200 --> 00:42:01.046
you can already see that this is
definitely a Java application.

00:42:01.046 --> 00:42:03.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:05.830
But why?

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:07.980
Because the heap is
going up, up, up.

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:09.480
And then there's
garbage collection.

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:11.650
And then you go up again.

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:13.620
But the good news
here is obviously

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:15.390
that there's no
memory leaks, right?

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:17.557
Because otherwise, you would
see this thing going up

00:42:17.557 --> 00:42:20.040
all the time and eventually
get OOM killed, right?

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:24.060
So that's basically what you
can get from this whole thing.

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:25.870
Now, the last thing
I want to show you,

00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:29.190
which is really, really awesome,
is by adding some debugging

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:31.210
agents into your application.

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:33.840
Now, what happens if you
are in a pinch, really,

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:36.610
really need to figure out
what happened to your app,

00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:39.930
why is it not working, what
it is that we've done wrong,

00:42:39.930 --> 00:42:41.700
you cannot reproduce
the issue locally.

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:43.300
You need to figure this out.

