00:48:49.877 --> 00:48:51.210
And somebody told me about this.

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:53.520
It's called binary
debugging with logs.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:54.353
What does that mean?

00:48:54.353 --> 00:48:55.770
This is what we
usually do, right?

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:57.150
We add a log message at first.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:00.190
At the beginning, say, I'm here.

00:49:00.190 --> 00:49:04.680
And then you add a message at
the very end to say, it works.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:06.650
If it doesn't get to
"it works," that means

00:49:06.650 --> 00:49:08.730
it broke somewhere
in the middle.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:10.925
Then you add a message
in the middle somewhere.

00:49:10.925 --> 00:49:12.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:12.260 --> 00:49:15.400
Maybe it broke here, right?

00:49:15.400 --> 00:49:18.710
And every time you do this, we
actually output the log for you

00:49:18.710 --> 00:49:20.420
